<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-leagues">
  <title>Watching soccer---the English leagues</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"Football: it's a funny old game."</p>
      <p>--- Jimmy Greaves</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The English soccer league, founded in 1888-89, is the oldest in the world and provides a long historical
      record for studying competition trends.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> Have draw rates changed? Has home advantage shifted? Are goals now rarer? How can team
      histories and within-season progress be shown effectively?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> The <c>engsoccerdata</c> package.</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> Match results summarized in 12 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-football-history">
    <title>History of the English football leagues since 1888</title>

    <p>
      The league evolved from one division to a four-tier structure with multiple reorganizations, including the
      formation of the Premier League in 1992.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-football-draws">
    <title>How have numbers of draws changed?</title>

    <p>
      In the top tier, draw rates rose early, stabilized for long periods, and then changed modestly after rules
      awarding three points for a win were introduced.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socd">
      <image source="images/socD-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Rate of draws over the years in the English top tier with a GAM smooth.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Comparison with Italy and Spain shows distinct national trajectories and timing differences around points-rule
      changes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socdf">
      <image source="images/socDf-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Smooths of draw rates for top tiers in Italy (dark blue), England (light blue), and Spain (red).</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-football-home-adv">
    <title>How big is home advantage?</title>

    <p>
      Home advantage can be measured as the share of points won at home. A consistent 3-points-for-win convention
      enables comparisons through time.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socha">
      <image source="images/socHA-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Proportions of points won at home over time in the various tiers of the English league.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Lower tiers show long-run decline in home advantage. The 2020-21 top-tier drop aligns with pandemic-era games
      played without fans.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-sochat">
      <image source="images/socHAt-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Proportions of points won at home for every team for every season.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Team-level results include striking outliers, including seasons with almost no away success and rare reverse
      home/away patterns.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-football-goals">
    <title>Have the numbers of goals scored gone down?</title>

    <p>
      Goals per game were much higher in the earliest years, then declined sharply, with historical jumps around
      rule changes and later stabilization near modern levels.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socg">
      <image source="images/socG-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>The numbers of goals per game for the four tiers (offside-law change marked with a red dashed line).</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-football-teams">
    <title>How well have teams performed over the years?</title>

    <p>
      Overall season rankings across tiers support long-run team trajectory comparisons. Ghosted backgrounds with
      highlighted teams help preserve context.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-teambps">
      <image source="images/teamBPS-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Comparing the three South Coast teams: Bournemouth, Portsmouth, and Southampton.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-teambpsq">
      <image source="images/teamBPSq-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Comparing Bournemouth, Portsmouth, and Southampton on a square root scale.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The square-root rank scale emphasizes differences near the top of the pyramid more strongly than linear ranks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-football-wormcharts">
    <title>Wormcharts show league progress over a season</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Wormcharts track cumulative points relative to season averages, revealing how teams separate during a
        campaign and highlighting decisive phases.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-worm15">
        <image source="images/worm15-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Team performances in points above/below average in 2015-16.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-worm82">
        <image source="images/worm82-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Team performances in points above/below average in season 1982-83.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-worm3768">
        <image source="images/worm3768-1.png"/>
        <caption>
          <p>
            Wormcharts for the two seasons with extreme point ranges for the English 1st Division Championship with
            22 teams and two points for a win between 1919-20 and 1980-81.
          </p>
        </caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Draw rates rose from very early low levels but changed little on average over the last
        forty years. Home advantage has declined, especially in lower tiers. Goals per game have been fairly steady
        for around fifty years. Wormcharts provide a clear season-level progression view.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> Would pre-1976 goal-difference ranking have changed outcomes? Which teams have
        longest stays in each tier? Which move between tiers most often?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-football-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Time-series graphics should mark major rule/context changes with guides.</p></li>
        <li><p>Highlighting specific cases with size, colour, and layering is effective.</p></li>
        <li><p>Transformations can emphasize different parts of a ranking scale.</p></li>
        <li><p>Wormcharts are illuminating for league progress through a season.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
